APX.at = {"country":{"name":"Austria","tagline":"Grüner Veltliner on Danube terraces, Blaufränkisch in Pannonian heat, and a wine country reborn from scandal into one of Europe's most rigorous.","classification":"Austrian labels mix three layers, and the most useful is origin.\n\nDAC. Each of the 18 DACs fixes the grapes and style that may use the regional name; anything else from the same place is sold under the broader federal-state origin (Niederösterreich, Burgenland, Steiermark, Wien). A Kamptal DAC is therefore always Grüner Veltliner or Riesling, a Mittelburgenland DAC always Blaufränkisch, a Weststeiermark DAC usually Schilcher. Most DACs now use three tiers: Gebietswein (regional), Ortswein (village) and Riedenwein (single vineyard, labelled 'Ried' plus the site name), with stricter yields, later release dates and often hand harvesting higher up. A 2023 collective ordinance lets each region classify Rieden as Erste Lage and, at least five years later, Große Lage, with criteria including history, homogeneity of soil and climate, market value and critical reputation; the terms may appear only on DAC wines.\n\nQuality levels. Qualitätswein (including the light, unchaptalised Kabinett) and Prädikatswein, graded by must weight in degrees KMW (Klosterneuburger Mostwaage): Spätlese (19), Auslese (21), Eiswein, Strohwein or Schilfwein (straw- or reed-dried grapes) and Beerenauslese (25), Ausbruch (27) and Trockenbeerenauslese (30).\n\nPrivate classifications. In the Wachau, Vinea Wachau's Steinfeder (up to 11.5 percent alcohol), Federspiel (11.5 to 12.5) and Smaragd (above 12.5) describe ripeness for unchaptalised dry wines. The ÖTW (Österreichische Traditionsweingüter, founded 1991, 78 members by 2023) has used 'ÖTW Erste Lage' since the 2009 vintage for top Danube-region sites, and Styria's STK and Burgenland groups such as Pannobile and Renommierte Weingüter Burgenland apply their own rules."},"regions":[{"name":"Niederösterreich","slug":"niederosterreich","tagline":"The Danube's terraces and the Weinviertel's loess: Austria's white-wine heartland.","ids":["at-carnuntum","at-kamptal","at-kremstal","at-niederosterreich","at-thermenregion","at-traisental","at-wachau","at-wagram","at-weinviertel"]},{"name":"Burgenland","slug":"burgenland","tagline":"Pannonian heat, a steppe lake and Austria's greatest reds and sweet wines.","ids":["at-burgenland","at-eisenberg","at-leithaberg","at-mittelburgenland","at-neusiedlersee","at-rosalia","at-ruster-ausbruch"]},{"name":"Steiermark","slug":"steiermark","tagline":"Steep green hills on the Slovenian border, built for Sauvignon Blanc.","ids":["at-steiermark","at-sudsteiermark","at-sud-oststeiermark","at-weststeiermark"]},{"name":"Wien","slug":"wien","tagline":"A world capital with 588 hectares of vines and a tavern on every hill.","ids":["at-wien","at-wiener-gemischter-satz"]},{"name":"Bergland","slug":"bergland","tagline":"Small, ambitious vineyards in the Alpine provinces.","ids":["at-karnten","at-oberosterreich","at-salzburg","at-tirol","at-vorarlberg"]}],"items":{"at-burgenland":{"name":"Burgenland","cat":"PDO","region":"Burgenland","created":"1999 (EU reg.)","area_ha":11538,"styles":["red","white","sweet","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"All authorised varieties","summary":"The federal-state PDO for Burgenland, used for quality wine from any of its areas that falls outside the DAC rules, for example Cabernet blends, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and most sweet wines from beyond Rust and the Seewinkel DAC rules.","nerd_note":"Many of Burgenland's most famous red blends, such as Gernot Heinrich's Pannobile, appear under the Burgenland origin rather than a DAC.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-carnuntum":{"name":"Carnuntum","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","created":"DAC from 2019 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":812,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zweigelt, Blaufränkisch; Grüner Veltliner, Chardonnay, Weißburgunder","summary":"Carnuntum lies east of Vienna between the Danube and the Leitha hills, named after the Roman legionary town and provincial capital whose ruins dominate Petronell. Its 800-odd hectares of loess, gravel and limestone at Göttlesbrunn, Höflein, Prellenkirchen and on the Spitzerberg are warm and windy, ideal for red wine. Zweigelt is the signature (sold by a growers' group as 'Rubin Carnuntum'), and Blaufränkisch from the Spitzerberg's limestone is the region's most serious wine. The DAC (2019 vintage) covers Zweigelt, Blaufränkisch, Grüner Veltliner, Chardonnay and Weißburgunder, with cuvées at least two-thirds from these.","terroir":"Loess and gravel terraces, limestone on the Spitzerberg and Hundsheimer Berg; Pannonian warmth with constant wind.","producers":["Muhr-van der Niepoort","Markowitsch","Glatzer","Netzl","Pittnauer","Trapl","Grassl"],"nerd_note":"Dorli Muhr (Muhr-van der Niepoort) helped revive the Spitzerberg's half-abandoned limestone terraces for Blaufränkisch.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-eisenberg":{"name":"Eisenberg","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC from 2009 vintage (EU reg. 2011)","area_ha":525,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Blaufränkisch; Welschriesling only with a Ried name","summary":"Eisenberg is the southern Burgenland area around the Eisenberg hill and the villages of Deutsch-Schützen, Höll and Moschendorf, near the Hungarian border, with about 525 hectares. The 'iron mountain' has greenschist and iron-rich loam soils that give Blaufränkisch of rare finesse, with dark cherry, pepper and a mineral, slightly bloody note. Since 2023 the Reserve category has gone and the DAC focuses on Blaufränkisch, with Welschriesling allowed for Riedenwein. South Burgenland is also the home of Uhudler, a rustic wine from American hybrid grapes that has its own protected status.","terroir":"Greenschist (phyllite), iron-rich loam and clay on the Eisenberg and the Deutsch-Schützen hills; warmer and wetter than northern Burgenland.","producers":["Uwe Schiefer","Wachter-Wiesler","Krutzler","Kopfensteiner","Jalits","Faulhammer","Weinbau Stefan Zehetbauer"],"nerd_note":"Uhudler, made from American hybrid grapes, spent decades in legal limbo before Burgenland re-authorised it in the 2010s; it is now a regional cult.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-kamptal":{"name":"Kamptal","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","subregion":"Danube","created":"DAC from 2008 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":3567,"styles":["white"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Riesling","summary":"The Kamptal follows the little Kamp river from the Waldviertel down to the Danube plain, with about 3,570 hectares around Langenlois, Austria's largest wine town, and the villages of Zöbing, Gobelsburg, Kammern, Strass and Schönberg. Its fame rests on the Heiligenstein, a ridge of 270-million-year-old desert sandstone that gives some of Austria's greatest Rieslings, and on loess slopes such as the Lamm, Spiegel and Renner that give deep, spicy Grüner Veltliner. Cool air from the Waldviertel cools the vineyards on summer nights, keeping the wines fresh. The DAC (2008) covers Grüner Veltliner and Riesling, with Ortswein and Riedenwein tiers; the ÖTW classification of Erste Lagen began here and in Kremstal.","terroir":"Permian sandstone and conglomerate with volcanic fragments on the Heiligenstein; gneiss and amphibolite on the Gaisberg; loess on the Lamm, Spiegel and Käferberg.","producers":["Bründlmayer","Schloss Gobelsburg","Hirsch","Hiedler","Jurtschitsch","Fred Loimer","Allram","Birgit Eichinger","Ehn","Steininger"],"nerd_note":"Schloss Gobelsburg, run by Michael Moosbrugger, belonged to the Cistercians of Zwettl Abbey, and its 'Tradition' wines recreate nineteenth-century cellar methods.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-karnten":{"name":"Kärnten","cat":"PDO","region":"Bergland","created":"EU reg. 2004","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Mixed, often fungus-resistant varieties","summary":"Carinthia's PDO, registered in 2004, covers small vineyards on sunny, often lakeside slopes around the Wörthersee, Millstätter See, the Lavant valley and near St. Paul's abbey. Plantings have grown fast since 2000, mostly Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Blanc, Pinot Noir and fungus-resistant varieties.","terroir":"Alpine slopes with cool nights; frost risk.","rslug":"bergland"},"at-kremstal":{"name":"Kremstal","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","subregion":"Danube","created":"DAC from 2007 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":2242,"styles":["white"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Riesling","summary":"Kremstal surrounds the old wine town of Krems at the eastern exit of the Wachau, with about 2,240 hectares. West of the town, around Stein and on the Pfaffenberg, the soils are the same primary rock as the Wachau and the Rieslings are equally fine; east and north of Krems, around Rohrendorf, Gedersdorf and Senftenberg, deep loess terraces give rich, spicy Grüner Veltliner. South of the Danube the vineyards around Furth and Palt lie below the great baroque abbey of Göttweig. The DAC (2007) allows Grüner Veltliner and Riesling, with Ortswein and Riedenwein tiers.","terroir":"Gneiss and mica schist at Stein and Senftenberg, deep loess at Krems, Rohrendorf and Gedersdorf.","producers":["Nigl","Salomon Undhof","Malat","Stift Göttweig","Lenz Moser","Proidl","Buchegger","Mantlerhof","Weinzierl"],"nerd_note":"The Kremser Sandgrube, recorded in 1208, is the oldest documented vineyard name in Austria.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-leithaberg":{"name":"Leithaberg","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC red from 2008, white from 2009 (EU reg. 2009)","area_ha":2955,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Blaufränkisch (red); Weißburgunder, Chardonnay, Neuburger, Grüner Veltliner and blends (white)","summary":"Leithaberg covers the western shore of Lake Neusiedl and the slopes of the Leitha hills (Leithagebirge), with Jois, Winden, Breitenbrunn, Purbach, Donnerskirchen, Schützen, Eisenstadt, Großhöflein and St. Georgen. Its soils of Leitha limestone and mica schist give wines with a cool, chalky structure: Blaufränkisch that is fine-boned rather than heavy, and whites from Pinot Blanc, Chardonnay, Neuburger and Grüner Veltliner that are dry, mineral and lightly oaked at most. The DAC was a growers' initiative, launched for reds in 2008 and whites in 2009, and it explicitly asks for 'minerality' rather than power.","terroir":"Leitha limestone (fossil-rich coral and shell limestone) and mica schist and gneiss on the slopes of the Leithagebirge; the lake's warmth below.","producers":["Prieler","Birgit Braunstein","Kollwentz","Leo Hillinger","Rosi Schuster","Esterházy","Gernot und Heike Heinrich","Andi Kollwentz","Markus Altenburger"],"nerd_note":"A single ancient vine found in 2000 near St. Georgen, in the Leithaberg area, turned out to be a parent of Grüner Veltliner; it was propagated and is now grown again as 'St. Georgen'.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-mittelburgenland":{"name":"Mittelburgenland","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC from 2005 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":2026,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Blaufränkisch","summary":"Mittelburgenland, which calls itself 'Blaufränkischland', covers about 2,000 hectares around Deutschkreutz, Horitschon, Neckenmarkt and Lutzmannsburg on the Hungarian border. Sheltered from the north by the Ödenburg (Sopron) hills and open to Pannonian warmth, its deep, heavy clay soils give Blaufränkisch of dark fruit, firm tannin and spice. It was Austria's second DAC (2005 vintage) and is limited to Blaufränkisch, with Ortswein, Riedenwein and Reserve categories. Roland Velich's Moric, from very old vines at Lutzmannsburg and Neckenmarkt, showed that Mittelburgenland Blaufränkisch could be ethereal rather than merely powerful.","terroir":"Deep, heavy clay and loam with gravel and sand; warm and sheltered.","producers":["Moric","Gesellmann","Franz Weninger","Paul Kerschbaum","J. Heinrich","Iby","Juliana Wieder","Albert Gesellmann"],"nerd_note":"Franz Weninger also farms vines across the border in Hungary's Sopron, the same Blaufränkisch landscape split by the 1921 frontier.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-neusiedlersee":{"name":"Neusiedlersee","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC from 2011 vintage (sweet wines added 2020; PDO 1996)","area_ha":5959,"styles":["red","sweet","white","rose"],"grapes":"Zweigelt (dry DAC); white varieties for sweet DAC","summary":"Neusiedlersee covers the north and east shores of Lake Neusiedl, from Gols and Mönchhof to Podersdorf, Illmitz and Apetlon in the Seewinkel, about 6,000 hectares. The shallow steppe lake (315 square kilometres but under 2 metres deep), the salty Seewinkel ponds and the Pannonian heat create two great wine styles. In summer and early autumn, warm days produce juicy Zweigelt and powerful red blends around Gols, where the Pannobile growers (Heinrich, Nittnaus, Achs, Beck, Pittnauer, Preisinger, Renner) set the tone. Then autumn mists off the lake bring noble rot, and Illmitz and Apetlon make Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese of world class; the late Alois Kracher made them famous in the 1990s with his numbered 'Zwischen den Seen' and 'Nouvelle Vague' TBAs. The DAC (2011) is for Zweigelt, classic or Reserve; since 2020 sweet wines from white grapes also qualify.","terroir":"Sand, gravel, loam and black earth (Tschernosem); salty soils in the Seewinkel; the lake moderates frost and feeds autumn mist.","producers":["Kracher","Gernot und Heike Heinrich","Claus Preisinger","Paul Achs","Nittnaus","Judith Beck","Gerhard Pittnauer","Velich","Juris","Hans Tschida (Angerhof)","Gerhard Nekowitsch"],"nerd_note":"Gerhard Nekowitsch in Illmitz pioneered Schilfwein, sweet wine from grapes dried on mats woven from the lake's reeds.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-niederosterreich":{"name":"Niederösterreich","cat":"PDO","region":"Niederösterreich","created":"1999 (EU reg.)","area_ha":26732,"styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"All authorised varieties","summary":"The federal-state PDO for Lower Austria, used for quality wines from anywhere in its eight DAC areas that do not meet (or do not seek) DAC rules: a Kamptal Zweigelt or a Wachau Chardonnay, for example, is labelled Niederösterreich. With 26,732 hectares, it is by far Austria's largest origin.","nerd_note":"Austrian Sekt g.U. (with Klassik, Reserve and Große Reserve tiers since 2015) is often labelled with the Niederösterreich origin.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-oberosterreich":{"name":"Oberösterreich","cat":"PDO","region":"Bergland","created":"EU reg. 2004","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Mixed, often fungus-resistant varieties","summary":"Upper Austria's PDO (2004) covers a handful of vineyards along the Danube and in the Alpine foreland, with fresh whites and some sparkling wine.","terroir":"Alpine slopes with cool nights; frost risk.","rslug":"bergland"},"at-rosalia":{"name":"Rosalia","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC from 2017 vintage","area_ha":228,"styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Blaufränkisch, Zweigelt (red); rosé from one or more red varieties","summary":"Rosalia, Austria's smallest DAC at about 230 hectares, lies on the eastern slopes of the Rosalia hills in the Mattersburg district of northern Burgenland, between the Leithaberg and Mittelburgenland. Its cool, forested hills give Blaufränkisch and Zweigelt with freshness and spice. Rosalia is the only DAC with a protected rosé category, which recognises the area's long tradition of pink wines.","terroir":"Gravel, sand, loam and crystalline rock on the slopes of the Rosaliengebirge, cooler than the lake shore.","producers":["Weingut Stefan Wellanschitz","Weingut Kopfensteiner","Weingut Steiner"],"nerd_note":"The region takes its name from a chapel to St. Rosalia on the hilltop.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-ruster-ausbruch":{"name":"Ruster Ausbruch","cat":"DAC","region":"Burgenland","created":"DAC from October 2020","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"One or more white varieties: Furmint, Welschriesling, Weißburgunder, Chardonnay, Gelber Muskateller and others","summary":"Ruster Ausbruch is one of the world's great historic sweet wines, made only in the town of Rust on the western shore of Lake Neusiedl. Botrytised berries are picked by hand, berry by berry, and must reach at least 30 degrees KMW, the same as a Trockenbeerenauslese, but the traditional method of soaking or pressing the shrivelled berries in fresh must gives a wine with more acid lift and less cloying sweetness. In the seventeenth century Ausbruch was famous across Europe, and Rust is said to have paid part of the price of its freedom as a royal town in 1681 in barrels of it. A DAC since 2020, it is a tiny production carried by a handful of families who still keep storks nesting on their chimneys.","terroir":"Rust's slopes of Leitha limestone and schist face the lake, whose mists bring noble rot in autumn.","producers":["Feiler-Artinger","Ernst Triebaumer","Heidi Schröck","Wenzel","Giefing","Weinbau Gabriel"],"nerd_note":"Michael Wenzel and Heidi Schröck helped revive Furmint in Rust, the grape of Tokaji, which had almost disappeared from Austria.","rslug":"burgenland"},"at-salzburg":{"name":"Salzburg","cat":"PDO","region":"Bergland","created":"EU reg. 2004","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Mixed, often fungus-resistant varieties","summary":"The PDO for the province of Salzburg, registered in 2004, has only a few hectares, notably near Salzburg city; it exists mainly so that growers can sell quality wine with a local origin.","terroir":"Alpine slopes with cool nights; frost risk.","rslug":"bergland"},"at-steiermark":{"name":"Steiermark","cat":"PDO","region":"Steiermark","created":"1999 (EU reg.)","area_ha":5109,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"All authorised varieties","summary":"The federal-state PDO for Styria, covering quality wines that fall outside the three Styrian DACs, including reds such as Zweigelt and Blauburger and many sparkling wines.","nerd_note":"Styria is known for the Klapotetz, a wooden wind-rattle that scares birds from the ripening grapes.","rslug":"steiermark"},"at-sud-oststeiermark":{"name":"Vulkanland Steiermark","cat":"DAC","region":"Steiermark","created":"DAC from 2018 vintage (PDO 1996 as Süd-Oststeiermark)","area_ha":1657,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Welschriesling, Weißburgunder, Morillon, Grauburgunder, Riesling, Gelber Muskateller, Sauvignon Blanc, Traminer","summary":"The Vulkanland in south-eastern Styria is a hilly landscape of extinct volcanoes, with castles on basalt plugs at Riegersburg and Kapfenstein, about 1,650 hectares of vines and a scattered pattern of small vineyards among orchards and fields. Volcanic basalt and tuff, mixed with sand and loam, give whites of spice and warmth; the Traminer of Klöch is the area's most famous speciality, intensely perfumed with rose petals. Neumeister in Straden and Winkler-Hermaden at Kapfenstein are the leading names. The DAC (2018 vintage) has three tiers; the region was called Süd-Oststeiermark until the change to Vulkanland.","terroir":"Basalt, volcanic tuff and ash over sand and clay; warmer and drier than Südsteiermark, with Pannonian influence.","producers":["Neumeister","Winkler-Hermaden","Frühwirth","Ploder-Rosenberg","Krispel","Domäne Kapfenstein"],"nerd_note":"Klöch's Weinbaumuseum and Traminer route celebrate a grape planted on the volcano's slopes for centuries.","rslug":"steiermark"},"at-sudsteiermark":{"name":"Südsteiermark","cat":"DAC","region":"Steiermark","created":"DAC from 2018 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":2798,"styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Morillon, Gelber Muskateller, Weißburgunder, Grauburgunder, Welschriesling, Riesling, Traminer","summary":"Südsteiermark is a landscape of steep, green hills along the Slovenian border south of Graz, with about 2,800 hectares around Gamlitz, Ehrenhausen, Leutschach, Spielfeld and the Sausal hills at Kitzeck. Vines grow on gradients of up to about 70 percent, among the steepest in Austria, and the Südsteirische Weinstraße winds along ridges where the vineyards of Austria and Slovenia touch. The wines are dry and aromatic: Sauvignon Blanc with gooseberry, elderflower and cassis-leaf intensity, the piercing grapey Gelber Muskateller, and Morillon (the local name for Chardonnay) that can be as complex as Burgundy from the best Rieden. The DAC (2018 vintage) has Gebietswein, Ortswein and Riedenwein tiers and requires hand harvesting.","terroir":"Opok (marl and calcareous sandstone), sand, gravel and coral limestone in the south; slate and schist in the Sausal, where Kitzeck's vineyards reach over 600 metres. Rainfall is higher than elsewhere in Austria, offset by steep drainage and warm days.","producers":["Tement","Polz","Gross","Sattlerhof","Lackner-Tinnacher","Wohlmuth","Hannes Sabathi","Maria und Sepp Muster","Werlitsch","Schauer","Peter Skoff","Erich und Walter Polz"],"nerd_note":"Tement's Zieregg vineyard straddles the border; the family's Slovenian holdings are bottled as Ciringa.","rslug":"steiermark"},"at-thermenregion":{"name":"Thermenregion","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","created":"DAC from 2023 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":1821,"styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Zierfandler, Rotgipfler, Weißburgunder, Chardonnay, Sankt Laurent, Pinot Noir","summary":"The Thermenregion runs south from Vienna along the edge of the Vienna Woods to Bad Vöslau, a landscape of spa towns (Baden bei Wien's hot springs give the name) and old monastery estates. Around Gumpoldskirchen, Pfaffstätten and Traiskirchen it grows two rare white grapes found almost nowhere else: Zierfandler (Spätrot) and Rotgipfler, which give rich, spicy, long-lived whites, dry or sweet. Further south, around Tattendorf, Sooß and Bad Vöslau, the hot gravel plain gives fine Sankt Laurent and Pinot Noir. The Thermenregion became Austria's eighteenth and last DAC with the 2023 vintage, with Gebietswein, Ortswein and Riedenwein tiers.","terroir":"Limestone and calcareous gravel at the foot of the Vienna Woods, warm Pannonian climate, plus the hot, stony Steinfeld plain to the south.","producers":["Johanneshof Reinisch","Stadlmann","Alphart","Zierer","Heinrich Hartl","Freigut Thallern","Weingut Biegler"],"nerd_note":"Freigut Thallern has belonged to the Cistercian abbey of Heiligenkreuz since 1141, one of the oldest continuously run wine estates in Austria.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-tirol":{"name":"Tirol","cat":"PDO","region":"Bergland","created":"EU reg. 1999","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Mixed, often fungus-resistant varieties","summary":"Tyrol's PDO (1999) covers small, sheltered vineyards in the Inn valley and its side valleys, including some of the highest in Austria.","terroir":"Alpine slopes with cool nights; frost risk.","rslug":"bergland"},"at-traisental":{"name":"Traisental","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","subregion":"Danube","created":"DAC from 2006 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":861,"styles":["white"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Riesling","summary":"The Traisental, south of the Danube around Traismauer, Herzogenburg and Nußdorf ob der Traisen, is the smallest of Lower Austria's Danube regions. Its vines grow on chalky conglomerate and gravel terraces that give Grüner Veltliner and Riesling with unusual tension and a firm, almost salty finish. The DAC dates from 2006.","terroir":"Calcareous conglomerate, gravel and loess on narrow terraces; cool air from the Alpine foothills to the south.","producers":["Markus Huber","Ludwig Neumayer","Tom Dockner","Stift Herzogenburg"],"nerd_note":"Unlike the crystalline Wachau and Kamptal, the Traisental's best sites are on limestone-rich conglomerate, a reason its Grüner often tastes leaner and more mineral.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-vorarlberg":{"name":"Vorarlberg","cat":"PDO","region":"Bergland","created":"EU reg. 1999","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Mixed, often fungus-resistant varieties","summary":"The westernmost PDO (1999), with a few hectares near Lake Constance and in the Rhine valley, close to Swiss and German neighbours.","terroir":"Alpine slopes with cool nights; frost risk.","rslug":"bergland"},"at-wachau":{"name":"Wachau","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","subregion":"Danube","created":"DAC from 2020 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":1285,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Riesling (Riedenwein only these two); 17 varieties for Gebietswein","summary":"The Wachau is a 36-kilometre stretch of the Danube between Melk and Krems where the river cuts through granite and gneiss hills, and its terraced vineyards, castles and baroque abbeys make it one of the loveliest wine landscapes in Europe (UNESCO World Heritage, 2000). Only about 1,285 hectares of vines grow here, mostly on narrow terraces held up by dry-stone walls, but they produce some of the world's finest dry Grüner Veltliner and Riesling.\n\nThe wines are dry and unchaptalised, and since 1983 the growers' association Vinea Wachau has graded them by ripeness: Steinfeder (light, up to 11.5 percent alcohol, named after a feathery grass on the terraces), Federspiel (11.5 to 12.5 percent, named after a falconry lure) and Smaragd (above 12.5 percent, named after the emerald lizards that bask on the walls). A Smaragd Riesling from the Achleiten or Kellerberg combines ripe stone fruit with a cool, stony core and can age for decades. Since the 2020 vintage the Wachau has been a DAC with three tiers: Gebietswein (17 varieties), Ortswein (nine varieties) and Riedenwein, reserved for Grüner Veltliner and Riesling from 157 named Rieden and always hand-picked.\n\nThe pioneers include Josef Jamek, who made dry Wachau wines famous in the 1950s and 1960s, and the generation of F.X. Pichler, Franz Hirtzberger, Emmerich Knoll and Franz Prager that followed. The Domäne Wachau cooperative in Dürnstein farms about a third of the area.","terroir":"Primary rock (gneiss, amphibolite, mica schist and granulite) on the terraces, loess and sand at the foot of the slopes. The Danube moderates temperatures, warm Pannonian air drifts up the valley from the east and cold air drains from the Waldviertel at night; the Spitzer Graben side valley is notably cooler. Irrigation is often needed on the thin terrace soils.","producers":["F.X. Pichler","Emmerich Knoll","Prager","Franz Hirtzberger","Leo Alzinger","Rudi Pichler","Nikolaihof","Tegernseerhof","Josef Jamek","Domäne Wachau","Pichler-Krutzler","Peter Veyder-Malberg","Martin Muthenthaler"],"nerd_note":"The Smaragd category is named after the emerald lizard that suns itself on the terrace walls; Steinfeder, Federspiel and Smaragd are trademarks of Vinea Wachau, not legal categories, so only members may use them.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-wagram":{"name":"Wagram","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","subregion":"Danube","created":"DAC from 2021 vintage (PDO 1996 as Donauland; renamed 2007)","area_ha":2411,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Roter Veltliner, Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Pinot Noir and others by tier","summary":"The Wagram is a long loess escarpment rising up to 40 metres above the Danube plain north of the river, between Krems and Vienna, together with the Klosterneuburg area south of the river. Its roughly 2,400 hectares are built on loess many metres deep, which gives Grüner Veltliner of creamy weight and spice. The region is also the home of Roter Veltliner, an old and difficult variety (unrelated to Grüner) that here gives rich, nutty whites. Bernhard Ott's biodynamic Grüner from Feuersbrunn made the Wagram's name. The area was called Donauland until 2007 and became a DAC from the 2021 vintage, with Gebietswein, Ortswein and Riedenwein tiers.","terroir":"Deep loess over gravel and marine sediments on a south-facing escarpment above the Danube.","producers":["Bernhard Ott","Karl Fritsch","Leth","Josef Fritz","Stift Klosterneuburg","Anton Bauer","Wimmer-Czerny"],"nerd_note":"The name Wagram comes from 'Wogenrain', the bank of the waves, referring to the ancient shoreline of the Danube.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-weinviertel":{"name":"Weinviertel","cat":"DAC","region":"Niederösterreich","created":"First DAC: from 2002 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":13730,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner (DAC); many others under Niederösterreich","summary":"The Weinviertel, the 'wine quarter' between the Danube, the Manhartsberg and the Czech and Slovak borders, is Austria's largest wine area with about 13,700 hectares, a third of the national vineyard, and in 2025 it produced 989,300 hectolitres. Rolling loess hills, sunflower fields and villages with cellar lanes (Kellergassen) define it. The Weinviertel became Austria's first DAC with the 2002 vintage and limited it to Grüner Veltliner in a fresh, fruity, peppery style (the famous 'Pfefferl'), with Reserve (from 2009) and Große Reserve (since 2020) for richer wines.\n\nThe region is diverse: Poysdorf in the north-east is Austria's Sekt base, Falkenstein has limestone cliffs and old castles, Röschitz near the Manhartsberg has granite, and the far north-east near the March river is warm enough for reds.","terroir":"Loess dominates, with limestone at Falkenstein and the Waschberg, granite near the Manhartsberg; a dry, Pannonian-continental climate.","producers":["Pfaffl","Ebner-Ebenauer","Graf Hardegg","Ingrid Groiss","Gruber Röschitz","Zull","Schwarzböck","Weinrieder","Christoph Hoch"],"nerd_note":"The Weinviertel was Austria's first DAC: its growers chose Grüner Veltliner as the region's sole flagship and launched the appellation with the 2002 vintage.","rslug":"niederosterreich"},"at-weststeiermark":{"name":"Weststeiermark","cat":"DAC","region":"Steiermark","created":"DAC from 2018 vintage (PDO 1996)","area_ha":655,"styles":["rose","sparkling","white"],"grapes":"Blauer Wildbacher (Schilcher); white varieties at Ort and Ried level","summary":"West Styria, south-west of Graz around Stainz, Deutschlandsberg, Ligist and Wies, is the land of Schilcher, a pale onion-skin to salmon rosé from the Blauer Wildbacher grape. Schilcher has piercing acidity and notes of redcurrant and rhubarb, a love-it-or-hate-it wine drunk locally with cold cuts in the Buschenschanken along the Schilcherweinstraße; modern producers also make it as sparkling wine and as a more structured Ried wine. About 650 hectares grow on gneiss and schist at up to 600 metres. The DAC (2018 vintage) requires hand harvest and covers Schilcher and selected white varieties.","terroir":"Gneiss and schist, often steep, at the edge of the Koralpe; cooler and wetter than the rest of Styria.","producers":["Christian Reiterer","Jöbstl","Domäne Müller","Langmann vlg. Lex","Weingut Strohmeier"],"nerd_note":"Blauer Wildbacher may only be labelled Schilcher if it comes from Styria; the word is protected.","rslug":"steiermark"},"at-wien":{"name":"Wien","cat":"PDO","region":"Wien","created":"1996 (EU reg.)","area_ha":588,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Grüner Veltliner, Riesling, Weißburgunder, Chardonnay, Gemischter Satz varieties, Zweigelt, Pinot Noir","summary":"Vienna's own PDO covers about 588 hectares within the city limits, on the Nussberg, Kahlenberg, Bisamberg and Maurer Berg. The wines, mostly white, are drunk largely in the city's Heurigen. Varietal wines and reds are labelled Wien, while field blends that meet the rules become Wiener Gemischter Satz DAC.","terroir":"Limestone, loess, gravel and flysch on the hills around the city; Pannonian warmth.","producers":["Wieninger","Mayer am Pfarrplatz","Rainer Christ","Zahel","Fuhrgassl-Huber","Hajszan Neumann","Edlmoser","Jutta Ambrositsch"],"nerd_note":"Vienna claims to be the only capital in the world with significant vineyards inside its borders.","contains":["Wiener Gemischter Satz"],"rslug":"wien"},"at-wiener-gemischter-satz":{"name":"Wiener Gemischter Satz","cat":"DAC","region":"Wien","created":"DAC from 2013 vintage","styles":["white"],"grapes":"At least three white varieties from one vineyard, planted together","summary":"Gemischter Satz means a 'mixed planting': vineyards where different varieties grow side by side and are picked and fermented together, as was normal before single-variety planting took over. Vienna kept the tradition alive, and Fritz Wieninger and a group of growers turned it into a quality wine and Austria's tenth DAC in 2013. At least three white varieties must be planted together in a Viennese vineyard; the largest may not exceed 50 percent and the third largest must reach at least 10 percent. The wines range from light Heuriger whites to complex single-vineyard blends from the Nussberg or Bisamberg.","terroir":"Vineyards on the Nussberg (limestone), Bisamberg (loess, gravel) and other Viennese hills.","producers":["Wieninger","Mayer am Pfarrplatz","Zahel","Rainer Christ","Jutta Ambrositsch"],"nerd_note":"Some Nussberg field blends include up to twenty varieties, among them old Viennese grapes such as Grüner Sylvaner, Rotgipfler and Zierfandler.","rslug":"wien"}}};
